link's awakening on Nintendo switch  maybe a remake but that doesn't mean  it's the same exact game if you've never  played the original or you need a  refresher here are 9 tips to help you  along your adventure if you haven't  noticed yet there are a lot more  seashells and Link's Awakening for the  Nintendo switch than the gameboy version  while these collectables don't have much  of a use in the beginning you'll  eventually come across the seashell  mansion this house lets you trade in the  seashells you've found for rewards I  don't want to spoil the prizes for  collecting seashells but they will prove  useful during your adventure so make  sure to stop by  one of the most useful additions to the  switch version are map markers sure the  island in Link's Awakening is much  smaller than Hyrule proper but there are  a lot of caves and areas you want to  return to later on the easiest way to  remember is to drop a marker like many  older Zelda games Link's Awakening has a  trading sequence that spans most of the  game I won't spoil the exact sequence  here but if you find or see an odd item  like this Yoshi plushie you should  probably get it in order to beat the  game you'll need to complete the entire  trading sequence so keep this in mind as  you progress this might go without  saying but if you're having trouble  finding a key in a dungeon listen for  your compass to chime there are a lot of  keys that you won't find in chests so if  you enter a room and hear a chime that  means there's a key waiting to be found  [Music]  980 rupees in order to get the bow might  seem steep but there are some quick ways  to get a ton of money in Link's  Awakening the most efficient  money-making mini game is called rapids  raid once you get to this part of the  map talk to this guy for 100 rupees you  can either race or raid while you should  do both we recommend starting with the  raid on most runs we made 200 rupees and  refilled our supplies if you're unsure  about what to do or where to go next  check the memory screen by pressing X  with the map open this tracks speeches  from The Owl and other key characters as  well as the amount of locations and  collectables you found unlike in most  Zelda games if you die in Link's  Awakening a bottled ferry won't  automatically revive you instead you'll  need crazy Tracey's secret oink mint you  can buy the medicine directly from her  but keep in mind the price does  fluctuate you can also find the medicine  in certain dungeons as well so keep a  lookout  [Music]  if you're a completionist make sure to  check the shop and the crane game house  frequently as you purchase or win  different items new ones will take their  place so if you're having trouble  finding the last few seashells or heart  pieces then stop by may village it's  also worth stopping in at the shop if  you're low on arrows bombs or magic  powder Link's Awakening DX  added a secret dungeon called the color  dungeon and it's back for the switch  version there's a useful prize in it for  those who managed to find it and  complete it I won't say where it is or  what you'll get but if you're having  trouble finding the dungeon maybe stop  by made villages library and read up on  the island there you have it those are  some of the tips we found useful if you  have any advice for new or returning  players feel free to leave them in the  comments thank you so much for watching  and stay tuned game spot for more of  zelda links awakening  [Music]
 

